{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 18, :comments ["<b>And you shall lay your hand upon him.</b> 'Your hand' — one hand in meaning; yet in the actual deed it is written: 'And he laid his hands upon him.' Rashi explains: more than he was commanded, etc. According to his words the question is hard: how did Moses transgress the prohibition of adding to the commandments? We find another such laying on of hands as well, for it says (Leviticus 1:4): 'And he shall lay his hand upon the head of the burnt-offering' — 'his hand,' one hand in meaning — yet in the actual deed it is written (Leviticus 16:21): 'And Aaron shall lay both his hands upon the head of the live goat.' And above, in the portion of וַיִּקְרָא, we explained that it is all one matter: for the leaders of the people must accept upon themselves on the condition that they be an atonement and a guilt-offering for the entire people, as it is written (Deuteronomy 1:13): 'And I will set them at your head,' and our Rabbis of blessed memory learned from here (Deuteronomy Rabbah 1:9) that the guilt of the people hangs upon their leaders. And just as the laying of a hand upon the sacrifice is to place the trace and taint of the one who leans upon that which is leaned upon — namely, the sacrifice — so too this laying on of a hand is to place the trace and taint of all Israel upon their leaders, to say that the guilt of the people hangs upon them. Now, in an ordinary sacrifice of a sinner there are two kinds of laying on of hands, for the sinner in the act of his sin is double, since there is no act without a preceding thought. Therefore concerning the scapegoat it says: 'And Aaron shall lay both his hands' — for it comes to atone for the sin of deed and the sin of thought. But the burnt-offering comes for the sin of thought alone; therefore it says: 'And he shall lay his hand upon the head of the burnt-offering' — 'his hand,' one hand in meaning." "So too the leaders of Israel — they bear the iniquity of the congregation, whether they sin in deed or whether they sin in thought, for it is in their power to protest and they do not protest. Now it is true that not every shepherd and leader is held accountable for the iniquity of another when he sins only in thought, for 'the hidden things belong to HaShem our Elohim,' and who knows the hidden treasures of his fellow, as Rashi explained in the portion of נִצָּבִים (Deuteronomy 29:28). But if the shepherd is 'a man in whom is spirit,' who knows how to walk against the spirit of each and every one like Joshua, and he knows to discern all the dispositions of every man — then it is just that he be held accountable even for the sin of thought, since his eyes, through the holy spirit upon him, see everything that arises in the spirit of his fellow, whether good or whether evil, and it was in his power to protest against it. And concerning this it says here: 'Take for yourself Joshua the son of Nun, a man in whom is spirit' — who is able to walk against the spirit of each one — 'and you shall lay your hand upon him.' And it uses 'your hand' for an inclusive sense, for here it speaks only of the iniquity of the generation that arises upon their spirit, the trace of which shall hang upon him — and by a kal va-chomer all the more so for the sin of deed, which shall hang upon him. And there was no need to say that he should also lay the second hand upon him, to place upon him the trace of the sin of deed, for that is a kal va-chomer; and there was no need to command concerning the second hand, for Moses would surely do so of himself and would learn the kal va-chomer from the one hand that leans upon him with regard to all that arises upon their spirit — since it says 'and you shall lay your hand' immediately after 'a man in whom is spirit.' Therefore at the time of the deed Moses did so, and learned the second hand by a kal va-chomer from the one, and laid both his hands upon him." "Another interpretation: since it says at the end of the matter: 'as HaShem spoke by the hand of Moses' — Moses would not change a thing, and would have leaned upon him one hand and not two. And what is written: 'And he laid his hands upon him' — its explanation is this: that Moses handed over to Joshua two kinds of governance. The first is governance in Torah and commandments; the second is civil governance. And concerning them it says: 'who shall go out before them and who shall come in before them and who shall bring them out and who shall bring them in,' and from here it appears that two modes of governance he handed over to him. Concerning governance according to the Torah and the commandment it says: 'who shall bring them out and who shall bring them in,' and 'bring them out' carries the sense of 'by force,' for we find the language of going out and coming in in matters of halakhah, as it is written: 'I am no longer able to go out and come in' (Deuteronomy 31:2) — Rashi explained it as a matter of halakhah. And he told him: govern them, compelling them with a strong hand regarding the Torah and the divine service; just as Moses had power over them with both hands, so he laid both his hands upon Joshua, to say that with all his strength he shall rule over them, compelling them to keep the Torah, and he shall bring them out and bring them in in matters of halakhah with all force and might — for in this matter it is not applicable to say 'the left hand pushes away and the right hand draws near,' for who would permit [relaxing] the lofty portion? And this is the matter entrusted to his two hands, to say that with all his strength he shall guide them on the path of HaShem." "But concerning civil governance for the ordering of the situation, it says: 'who shall go out before them and who shall come in before them,' to say that with gentleness he shall conduct them and shall not deal with them with coercion. And this is the matter upon which he leaned only one hand, to say that in this governance — since they are a stiff-necked people and their way is to kick against their leaders — therefore the left hand shall push away and the right hand draw near, and he shall go out and come in before them and they shall follow after him of themselves, and not like one who takes hold of his hand and leads him by force. And it may be that for this reason Moses said to Joshua: 'for you shall come with this people' (Deuteronomy 31:7), while the Holy One, blessed be He, said to Joshua: 'for you shall bring the children of Israel' (Deuteronomy 31:23) — 'bring' carrying the sense of 'by force' — and there is no contradiction, for Moses was speaking concerning the matter of civil governance, and concerning that he said: 'for you shall come with this people' — everything according to their understanding and counsel, as Rashi explained in its place. But the Holy One, blessed be He, was urging him regarding the governance of the Torah, and concerning that He said: 'for you shall bring' — as will be explained, with the help of HaShem, in the portion of וַיֵּלֶךְ (Deuteronomy 31:7)." "And since it says in the portion of וָאֵרָא (Exodus 6:13): 'And he commanded them concerning the children of Israel' — Rashi explained: he commanded them to lead them gently — hear from this that wherever the language of command is used here, it speaks of civil governance, which needs to be conducted with gentleness. For 'command' (צַו) means nothing but urging, and we learn this from what is written: 'And charge Joshua, etc.' (Deuteronomy 3:28), for the leader requires urging all the more in civil governance, which is their way to kick against their leaders. Therefore when He said to him: 'Take for yourself Joshua the son of Nun, a man in whom is spirit' — who is able to walk against the spirit of each one, that is, in the matter of civil governance, to subdue all whose spirit is haughty against him — He said: 'and you shall lay your hand upon him,' meaning one hand, to say to him that one hand shall push away the rebels and one draw them near. And concerning this it says: 'and you shall set him before Eleazar and you shall charge him before their eyes.' And we learn 'and you shall charge him' from 'and he charged them': just as 'and he charged them' said there is with gentleness and speaks of civil governance, so too 'and you shall charge him' said here is with gentleness — that is, with one hand." "And afterward He said, 'And you shall place of your splendor upon him' — regarding the matter of prophecy, to lead them through it according to the Torah and the commandment. And in this He did not mention one hand, for whoever gives with a generous eye gives prophecy, and likewise regarding the matter of Torah and commandment — surely he will strengthen himself for their observance with all force and might. And concerning this He said: 'And before Elazar the priest he shall stand and inquire of him by the judgment of the Urim before HaShem' — for every sealed and hidden matter in the Torah — 'at his word they shall go out and they shall come' in matters of law. 'And Moses did… and he laid both his hands upon him' — that is, regarding the conduct of the Torah, as stated. 'And he commanded him as HaShem commanded by the hand of Moses' — for 'and he commanded him' indicates political governance, as stated: he did as HaShem commanded by the hand of Moses, that is, with one hand, as stated — even though there is an etnaḥta under the word 'and he commanded him,' nevertheless words of Torah are like a hammer that shatters a rock, expounded for all their purposes. And we may say according to the plain sense that the giving of the splendor did not mention one hand, for there is no limitation before HaShem, blessed be He; rather, HaShem desired to make him a full and overflowing vessel of the blessing of HaShem — and so he did, in the manner that has been explained."], :book :numbers, :voice :kli-yakar, :lang :en, :chapter 27, :notes {1 "The word רוּחַ carries throughout the double sense of 'spirit/disposition' and 'wind'; the translation preserves 'spirit' consistently to maintain the wordplay intact.", 5 "The word יִכְפֵּם appears to be a form of כפה, 'to coerce/strengthen oneself'; read hyper-literally as given. The phrase כְּפַטִּישׁ יְפוֹצֵץ סָלַע (Jeremiah 23:29) is rendered verbatim."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T13:43:55.311686209Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}